# /etc/fstab: where every part of this machine comes from.
#
# Nothing below is a disk. The tree you are standing in was handed to your
# browser at boot and lives in the memory of this tab, which is why `rm -rf /`
# is survivable and why everything you write is gone on reload.

# <source>       <mount point>              <type>     <options>
manifest.json    /                          memfs      rw,volatile,lang=en
registry         /usr/bin                   autofs     ro,generated
pi:/api          /proc                      nfs        ro,soft,synthetic-ok
localStorage     /home/guest/.bash_history  browserfs  rw,persistent


NOTES

manifest.json
    Every .txt you can `cat` was written in the repository, under content/en
    or content/pt, and baked into the JavaScript at build time. That is also
    where `ls -l` gets its dates: the mtime of a file is the date of the last
    commit that touched it. Remounting is what `lang` does -- same tree, other
    language, same paths, so you keep the directory you were standing in.

registry
    /usr/bin is not a directory anyone maintains. It is written at boot from
    the list of commands that actually exist, so it cannot drift from `help`.

pi:/api
    /proc is the only mount that leaves your browser. Uptime, temperature,
    memory, disks and processes are read from the real /proc of the Raspberry
    Pi answering you, over HTTPS, when you ask for them. If that machine is
    down, the commands say so instead of inventing a number. If it is up but
    not Linux -- when I run this on a laptop -- the reply is marked
    `synthetic: true` and the terminal prints the warning.

localStorage
    Your history and your preferences, font size and CRT, are written by this
    browser and read by this browser. They are never sent anywhere. Clearing
    this site's data removes them. See /etc/privacy.
